        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          No. Standard laminate flooring is not waterproof: its core is high-density fiberboard, and water that sits on the seams soaks into the raw edges and makes them swell, permanently. Newer water-resistant laminates with sealed edges and treated cores can tolerate standing surface water for a rated window, commonly somewhere between 24 and 72 hours, but no laminate survives a flood, a slow leak underneath, or moisture rising through the subfloor. The rest of this page is about the difference between those situations, because that difference decides which rooms laminate belongs in.
        </p>

        <div id="why-not-waterproof" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Isn't Standard Laminate Waterproof?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate is a photograph of wood sealed under clear plastic, glued to a board made of wood fiber. From the top down, a plank has a melamine wear layer, a printed decor paper, a high-density fiberboard (HDF) core that makes up most of the thickness, and a backing or balancing layer underneath. The wear layer on top is genuinely water resistant. A puddle sitting in the middle of a plank, away from any joint, does almost nothing to it. The weak points are the core and the edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every plank's tongue and groove is milled through raw fiberboard, so each seam in the floor is a line of exposed core. When water sits over a seam, capillary action pulls it down into the joint and into those wood fibers. The fibers swell, the edges of neighboring planks rise, and you get the telltale ridges along the seams that installers call peaking or edge swell. Water that gets under the floor does the same thing from the bottom, where there is no protective layer at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The swelling is the part that catches people out. Solid hardwood that cups after getting wet can partly flatten again as it dries. Swollen HDF usually does not. As the compressed fibers absorb water and expand, the resin bonds holding them together break, so the edge stays thicker even after the plank is bone dry. A floor that has peaked at the seams generally needs those planks replaced, not dried, which is why the question of waterproofing matters more for laminate than for most floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is also why the industry draws a line between water-resistant and waterproof. On flooring packaging, water-resistant means the floor tolerates spills that are cleaned up promptly. Waterproof, when it appears on laminate, means the product passed a test for standing water on the surface for a stated number of hours. Neither term covers water coming from below or a room full of water. Our guide to the <a href="/best-laminate-flooring/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best laminate flooring</a> scores water performance separately from durability for exactly that reason.
          </p>

